The independent review confirms the supplied suite and CLI contract, but it also found five specification branches the visible cases do not exercise: matched-name coverage, list prefixes/rests, mixed open/closed tuple products, open unions, and record-field subject types. These are material because the packet describes them explicitly, so I’m extending the boundary suite with small source programs for each before changing the analyzer.

`★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────`
- A matched-name pattern (`== name`) is not a wildcard: even over `bool`, the compiler cannot know which one value it covers, so it must contribute zero certain coverage.
- “Finite type” and “finite dimension” differ. `(int, bool)` is globally infinite, but the Boolean axis can still be completely partitioned while the integer axis is covered by a binder.
- List exhaustiveness is structural: `[]` plus `[_, ..]` covers all lengths without ever calculating a length.
