The root-cause review agrees with the suppression hypothesis and rules out a type-inference mistake. A separate specification audit is still running.

I’m expanding the regression suite only with behavior stated explicitly in `PACKET.md`: string list patterns, compound relational patterns, fresh arm bindings, matched-name coverage, and `switch` inside an arm guard. This distinguishes a narrow visible-case patch from missing language constructs that held-out cases are likely to exercise.

`★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────`
The checker uses two kinds of coverage representation: finite enumeration for closed types and integer intervals for `int`. Compound relational patterns belong in the interval path; treating `>= 4 and <= 7` as an unknown pattern incorrectly turns valid partitions into both `vacuous_arm` and `switch_inexhaustive`.
